{"name":"Revolver","hex":"#37363f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"revolver","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A handgun with a revolving chamber enabling several shots to be fired without reloading.","Any (personal) firearm with such a mechanism."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/revolver"}],"entries":[{"title":"Revolver","blocks":[{"text":"A revolver is a repeating handgun with at least one barrel and a revolving cylinder containing multiple chambers for firing. Because most revolver models hold six cartridges before needing to be reloaded, revolvers are commonly called six shooters or sixguns. Due to their rotating cylinder mechanism, they may also be called wheel guns."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/M%26Prevolver.jpg/330px-M%26Prevolver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revolver"},{"title":"Revolver (Beatles album)","blocks":[{"text":"Revolver is the seventh studio album by the English rock band the Beatles. It was released on 5 August 1966, accompanied by the double A-side single \"Eleanor Rigby\" and \"Yellow Submarine\". The album was the Beatles' final recording project before their retirement as live performers and marked the group's most overt use of studio technology to date, building on the advances of their late 1965 release Rubber Soul. It has since become regarded as one of the greatest and most innovative albums in the history of popular music, with recognition centred on its range of musical styles, diverse sounds and lyrical content."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/ec/Revolver_%28album_cover%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revolver_(Beatles_album)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Bullet_coming_from_S%26W.jpg/500px-Bullet_comi